course: everyday
lesson: 30
title: Thinking like an animal
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Animals do not think like people: much of what they do comes from instincts that helped their wild ancestors survive, and if you know the reason behind a behaviour you can predict what an animal will do next.
explain: Hunters such as dogs and cats chase what runs and pounce on what moves, so a running child or a fluttering hand can switch on the chase, while a still, quiet person switches it off.
explain: Prey animals such as horses, cows and rabbits survive by noticing danger and escaping, so a sudden noise or a shape they cannot see can make them bolt, kick or bite in fright.
explain: Parents of every kind defend their young, which is why a swooping magpie, a mother bear or a cow with a calf is more dangerous than the same animal at another time.
explain: For example, a fluffy young bird hopping on the lawn is usually a fledgling learning to fly; its parents are close by, still feeding it, so the kindest thing is to leave it alone and keep cats indoors.
explain: A boy in Australia walked too close to a magpie's nest in spring; the bird swooped again and again and cut his ear with its beak.
explain: A girl in Oregon cornered a frightened cat behind a shed to stroke it; with no way out, it scratched and bit her.
fact: Many animal behaviours are instincts inherited from wild ancestors.
fact: Hunting animals have a strong instinct to chase things that run away.
fact: Pet cats still have a hunting instinct even when they are well fed.
fact: Cats often bring their catch back to their home, the place where they feel safe.
fact: Prey animals escape danger by running, kicking or hiding.
fact: A fledgling is a young bird with feathers that has left the nest and hops on the ground while its parents still feed it.
fact: Most birds will not abandon a chick because a person has touched it.
fact: A baby bird with no feathers on the ground has fallen out too early; if the nest is in reach, an adult can gently put it back.
fact: Young animals that seem to be alone, such as fledglings or bear cubs, usually have a parent close by.
fact: A rattlesnake's rattle is a warning that helps it avoid being stepped on and avoid a fight, so it does not waste its venom.
fact: A honeybee's barbed sting gets stuck in the stretchy skin of mammals, but not in the hard bodies of other insects.
fact: An animal in pain may bite even a person it loves.
fact: An animal woken by a sudden touch may snap in fright before it knows who you are.
fact: Many animals guard their territory, such as a dog behind its own fence or gate.
fact: A trapped or cornered animal is much more likely to bite or scratch.
fact: Many animals attack to protect their babies, nest or food, not because they are mean.
exercise: Why does running away make a dog more likely to chase you? | Running switches on its hunting instinct to chase what flees | Dogs think running people are carrying food or treats for them | Running makes you smell of fear only | Dogs only chase people they already know
exercise: A well-fed pet cat still catches mice. Why? | The hunting instinct does not switch off when the cat is full | The cat is secretly starving | The cat is trying to make you buy more food from the shop for it | Mice are the only food cats can digest
exercise: Why does a cat often bring a mouse home instead of eating it where it caught it? | Home is where it feels safe to deal with its catch | It wants to show the mouse round the house first | It wants to show off to the other cats in the street | It wants to swap it for cat food
exercise: You find a feathered young bird hopping on the lawn. Why should you usually leave it there? | It is a fledgling, and its parents are nearby still feeding it | It is already fully grown and needs no help | Its parents will reject it if it smells of people, so never touch it | Birds on the ground are never in any danger
exercise: Is it true that a mother bird abandons a chick a person has touched? | No, most birds will not abandon a chick for that | Yes, every bird smells people at once and leaves for good | Yes, but only in the spring | Yes, but only if you hold it for long
exercise: Why might a horse kick when a child creeps up silently from behind? | As a prey animal, it reacts to a surprise it cannot see | It knows the child is playing a trick and wants to stop it | Horses only kick children they dislike | It is hoping the child has an apple
exercise: Why does a rattlesnake rattle instead of biting straight away? | A warning keeps big animals off without wasting venom | Rattling is how it calls its babies back to hide in the nest | It rattles because it is happy | It rattles to warm up its tail
exercise: A honeybee stings a wasp and lives, but dies after stinging a person. Why? | Its barbed sting sticks in stretchy skin but slides out of a hard insect body | Wasps have no blood for the sting | People are much more poisonous than wasps | Bees only die when they sting in the summer
exercise: The family dog has a hurt paw and snaps when Tom touches it. Why? | Pain makes even a loving animal bite to protect itself | The dog has stopped loving Tom | Dogs bite whenever they are touched, even when they are well | The dog thinks Tom is a stranger
exercise: Why can a dog that is friendly at the park bark and snap behind its own garden gate? | It is guarding its home territory | Gates make dogs angry for no reason | It cannot see people through gates | It is a different dog when at home
exercise: Why do Australian magpies swoop people mainly in spring? | It is when they have eggs and chicks to protect | They are hungrier in spring | They are learning to fly in spring and keep crashing into people | Spring sunshine hurts their eyes
exercise: Why is a bear that has been fed by tourists more dangerous than a wild one? | It has learned to link people with food and comes close | Human food makes bears grow much stronger, faster and bigger | Fed bears lose their sense of smell | Tourists teach bears to be angry
exercise: Why does a cat pounce on your wiggling toes under a blanket? | The movement looks like small prey to its hunting instinct | It thinks your toes are other cats | It is cold and wants to warm up | It wants to stop you from sleeping, so you will get up and feed it
exercise: A dog chases a cyclist but ignores a man standing still. What explains this? | Movement away from it triggers the chase | It likes bicycles far more than it likes people | The man standing still smells like its owner | Dogs only chase things with wheels on, never people
test: Charlie's cat leaves a dead mouse on the doormat in Bristol. What is the best way to understand it? | It is the cat's natural hunting behaviour, so he should not punish it | The cat wants to make him ill | The cat is hungry and asking for its dinner to be put out much earlier | The cat is angry with him
test: A child in Auckland finds a featherless baby bird on the ground below a nest she can reach. What is best? | Ask an adult to put it gently back in the nest | Leave it on the ground for the parents to feed | Take it home and feed it bread and milk | Leave it out because its parents will reject it now
test: Which is the best reason for being «a tree» when a strange dog runs at you? | A still person gives the dog nothing to chase | Trees are scary to dogs | Dogs cannot see things that stand still, so you disappear | It makes the owner come faster
test: Why should you approach a sleeping dog by calling its name rather than touching it? | Waking with a fright can make it snap before it knows who you are | Sleeping dogs are always angry | Dogs never wake up when touched | It will remember the touch for the rest of its life and never trust you
test: What would happen to a fledgling if a kind boy took it home to «save» it? | It would lose its parents, who are best at feeding and teaching it | It would grow faster than in the wild | It would learn to fly sooner indoors | Nothing, fledglings do not need parents
test: Which is true? | A full cat may still hunt, because the instinct is separate from hunger | Cats hunt only when they are hungry | Pet cats have lost their hunting instinct after living with people for so long | Cats bring mice home to poison their owners
test: Why is a cow with a new calf more dangerous than the same cow without one? | Parents instinctively defend their young | Cows with calves are always hungry and cross | Having a calf makes its mother see less well | Cows charge only when they feel very tired
test: A rattlesnake rattles and then a hiker backs away. How did the rattle help the snake? | It avoided a fight and kept its venom | It caught its dinner | It scared away its own babies | It called other snakes nearby to come and help it
test: A girl in Colorado sees two bear cubs with no mother in sight. Why is this a dangerous moment? | The mother is probably nearby and will defend her cubs | Cubs are more dangerous than adult bears, as they bite harder | Bear cubs always attack people | Lost cubs call wolves to help them
test: dog keeps chasing the cyclists, why | Instinct to chase things that run | He wants a bike | He thinks they are lost and wants to help | He's bored of walks
test: cat brought a mouse home, she's not even hungry | Cats hunt by instinct even when fed | She only does it when she's starving | She wants us to cook it for her dinner | She's sick and trying to tell us something
test: baby bird hopping on the ground, rescue it? | Probably a fledgling, parents still feed it | Yes, it's always lost | Take it home in a box | Put it back up and never touch it
test: our dog snapped at me when he hurt his paw | Animals in pain may bite even people they love | He doesn't love you any more | He was just playing | Dogs never feel pain in their paws, so it wasn't that
test: cat's stuck behind the shed, hissing, i wanna get it out | Leave it a way out and get an adult | Grab it quickly before it runs off again | Block the gap so it cannot run away from you | Poke it with a stick to make it move
test: magpie keeps swooping at me on the way to school | Walk away from its nest and cover your head | Wave your arms and shout at it | Throw stones at it | Stand still under its tree
test: Alex says animals only bite cos they're nasty. right? | No, they usually bite from fear or to protect something | Yes, some animals are just evil and bite people for fun | Yes, only nasty animals bite | Yes, unless they are pets
test: my nan says if u touch a baby bird the mum smells u and kicks it out the nest forever | That is a myth; most birds will not abandon a chick because a person touched it | She is right, the mother always smells your hands and abandons it | She is right, but only for blackbirds | She is right, so never look at nests
test: they're saying the Browns' cat brought in a dead rabbit cos they don't feed it enough | Probably wrong; well-fed cats still hunt by instinct and bring their catch home | It must be true, only hungry cats hunt | It proves the cat is dangerous to people | It means rabbits like going into houses
test: Lucy's dog bit her when it was hurt and people say it's a bad dog now, pass it on? | No; an animal in pain may bite even someone it loves, and the gossip is unfair | Yes, because any dog that bites is bad | Yes, so nobody visits Lucy's house | Yes, and say it bites everyone
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the hunting behaviour of well-fed domestic cats? | They retain a hunting instinct notwithstanding that they are well fed | They hunt only when deprived of food | They have lost all hunting instinct entirely through living with people | They hunt only birds and never mammals
exam: Identify the definition of a fledgling. | A young feathered bird that has left the nest and hops upon the ground whilst its parents still feed it | A newly hatched bird without feathers inside the nest | An adult bird that has lost the power of flight | A bird that has been abandoned by its parents
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning animals that run away. | Hunting animals possess a strong instinct to pursue that which flees | Hunting animals ignore anything that runs away | Running away always causes a hunting animal to stop | Only prey animals give chase to things that run
exam: It being given that an animal is in pain, determine the conduct that may be expected of it. | It may bite, even a person whom it loves | It will invariably remain gentle with its owner | It will seek to be lifted up and cuddled by its owner | It will display no change whatever in its behaviour
